---
// Terms (version dated 28 Oct 2026). Prices are never stated here: each
// written quote sets the price, any staged payments (amounts, milestones,
// due dates), how completed work is valued if a build is cancelled, and,
// if Stapled. hosts the site, the hosting and care cost, included work,
// domain costs and cancellation arrangements. Homepage previews are
// offered case by case. Earlier versions are kept privately, outside this
// repository, and each quote names the version that applies. Existing
// clients' agreements are unchanged. Three situations are kept apart:
// cancelling an unfinished build (settlement for work completed, then a
// cancellation handover within 14 days), paying for a finished build
// (finished = delivered and approved in writing), and cancelling hosting
// and care (optional, separate, under the quote). Ownership covers
// client-specific work; reusable components and third-party materials are
// licensed, not transferred. Only settled terms appear here;
// anything awaiting a decision is marked with a <ToConfirm> (visible in
// development, hidden in production, and caught by `npm run check:publish`).
import LegalLayout from "../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { contact, business } from "../data/config";

const lastUpdated = "28 October 2026";

const sections = [
  { id: "about", label: "About these terms" },
  { id: "who-for", label: "Business and personal buyers" },
  { id: "preview", label: "Homepage previews" },
  { id: "quotes", label: "Quotes and accepting them" },
  { id: "payment", label: "Paying for your website" },
  { id: "finished", label: "When your build is finished" },
  { id: "ownership", label: "What becomes yours, and when" },
  { id: "early", label: "Cancelling an unfinished build" },
  { id: "maintenance", label: "Hosting and care" },
  { id: "backups", label: "Backups" },
  { id: "own-hosting", label: "Using your own hosting" },
  { id: "handover", label: "Handover" },
  { id: "after-hosting", label: "After hosting ends" },
  { id: "domain", label: "Your domain and content" },
  { id: "late", label: "If a payment is late" },
  { id: "refunds", label: "Refunds and your rights" },
  { id: "cancel-14", label: "14-day cancellation (personal buyers)" },
  { id: "contact", label: "Questions and complaints" },
];
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Terms | Stapled."
  description="How buying a website from Stapled. works: written quotes, paying for your website, when it becomes yours, optional hosting and care, cancelling and handover."
  path="/terms/"
  heading="Terms"
  intro="How buying a website from me works, in plain English: what you pay, when it's yours, and how to leave."
  lastUpdated={lastUpdated}
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="about">
    <h2>About these terms</h2>
    <p>
      These terms are between you and {business.proprietor}, a sole trader
      trading as {business.tradingAs} ("I" or "me"). You can reach me at{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${contact.email}`}>{contact.email}</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      My full business details, including my{" "}
      {business.addressLabel.toLowerCase()}, are on the{" "}
      <a href="/business-details/">business details</a> page.
    </p>
    <p>
      They apply alongside the written quote I send you. Your quote says
      which version of these terms applies, by its date. When you accept a
      quote by email, the quote and that version of these terms make up our
      agreement. If you agreed a different arrangement with me before{" "}
      {lastUpdated}, that arrangement still applies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="who-for">
    <h2>Business and personal buyers</h2>
    <p>
      I work with businesses and with individuals. Some parts of these
      terms depend on which you are, because the law gives extra protection
      to people buying for personal use.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Buying for a business:</strong> you're buying mainly for your
        trade, business or profession. That includes sole traders and
        partnerships buying for their business.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Buying for personal use:</strong> you're buying mainly for
        purposes outside any trade or business. The law calls you a
        "consumer", and the parts marked "personal buyers" apply to you.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="preview">
    <h2>Homepage previews</h2>
    <p>
      For some projects, I may offer to put together a first look at how
      your homepage could feel and read, based on what you've told me. I
      don't offer one with every enquiry. If I do, it's free, and you're
      under no obligation to go ahead afterwards.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="quotes">
    <h2>Quotes and accepting them</h2>
    <p>Before any work starts, I'll email you a quote that sets out:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the agreed scope of the build, including any rounds of revisions</li>
      <li>
        the deliverables: the transferable source code and website assets,
        and design files only where the quote expressly includes them
      </li>
      <li>its total build price, and the value of each stage of the work</li>
      <li>how work on a partly completed stage is valued</li>
      <li>
        if you'd like to spread the cost, each payment amount, the stage it
        relates to, and when it's due
      </li>
      <li>
        whether you'd like me to host and look after the website (this is
        optional), and if so: what hosting and care costs, the work it
        includes, any domain costs, and how it can be cancelled
      </li>
      <li>whether you're buying for a business or for personal use</li>
      <li>which version of these terms applies</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      You accept a quote by replying to say so. Nothing is payable until you
      do. Any extra work you ask for later is quoted separately and only
      goes ahead if you agree.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="payment">
    <h2>Paying for your website</h2>
    <p>
      Your quote sets your website's total build price and the value of
      each stage. You can pay it in one go or, if we agree, in stages: your
      written quote sets out each payment amount, the stage it relates to,
      and when it's due.
    </p>
    <p>
      Payments are made by bank transfer. I'll send you the details with
      your quote. I'm not registered for VAT, so no VAT is added to any
      price.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you're paying in stages, you can pay the remaining balance at any
      time to finish paying early.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="finished">
    <h2>When your build is finished</h2>
    <p>
      Your build is finished when I've completed the agreed scope and any
      revisions included in your quote, delivered it to you for review, and
      you've approved it in writing (an email is fine). It isn't treated as
      approved just because time has passed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Once it's finished, any remaining payments are due as your quote sets
      out. Cancelling at that point doesn't replace paying for the finished
      build. If anything hasn't been done with reasonable care and skill,
      your rights in{" "}
      <a href="#refunds">refunds and your rights</a> still apply.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="ownership">
    <h2>What becomes yours, and when</h2>
    <p>Ownership passes to you in one of two ways:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Full payment:</strong> once you've paid the full build price,
        the finished website's deliverables listed in your quote.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cancellation settlement:</strong> if you cancel an unfinished
        build, once you've paid the agreed settlement, the work completed up
        to the cancellation, as described in{" "}
        <a href="#early">cancelling an unfinished build</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>In either case:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Work made for you:</strong> I transfer to you the ownership
        rights I hold in the design and code I created specifically for your
        website, and send you its deliverables.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>My reusable components:</strong> some parts of your website
        may be tools, code or design components I made before your project,
        or use across projects. I keep ownership of those, and you get
        permission to keep using them as part of your website, including if
        you move it to another host.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Third-party materials:</strong> some parts may be licensed
        from someone else, such as a stock photo, a font or a third-party
        service. They're not mine to transfer. They stay subject to their own
        licences, and I'll tell you about any restrictions before you accept
        your quote.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Nothing transfers before full payment or an agreed cancellation
      settlement has been paid.
    </p>
    <p>
      Paying in full doesn't end hosting and care. If I'm hosting your
      website, that carries on under its separate arrangement until it's
      cancelled as described in <a href="#maintenance">hosting and care</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="early">
    <h2>Cancelling an unfinished build</h2>
    <p>
      You can cancel the build at any time before it's finished, by emailing
      me. If you cancel before I've started any work, you owe nothing and
      I'll refund anything you've paid.
    </p>
    <h3>The cancellation settlement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>What you pay:</strong> the value of the work completed up to
        when you tell me you're cancelling, using the stage values in your
        quote and its method for a partly completed stage. You don't have to
        pay the whole remaining balance, and no part of the price is a
        non-refundable deposit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Payments you've already made</strong> count towards that
        amount. If you've paid more than the value of the work completed,
        I'll refund the difference. If you've paid less, you pay the
        difference.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>I'll confirm the settlement in writing,</strong> showing how
        it was worked out.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>The cancellation handover</h3>
    <p>
      Within 14 days of receiving the agreed settlement, I'll send you:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        the work completed up to the cancellation, as the deliverables listed
        in your quote, in its current state
      </li>
      <li>
        a note of its condition: what's finished, what isn't, any problems I
        know about, and that it may need more work before it can go live
      </li>
      <li>
        a list of any third-party materials it contains, whether their
        licences carry over to you, and anything I've had to remove or that
        you'd need to license yourself
      </li>
      <li>the content you gave me</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Ownership of the completed work passes as described in{" "}
      <a href="#ownership">what becomes yours, and when</a>. If the
      settlement isn't paid, the handover waits until it is (see{" "}
      <a href="#late">if a payment is late</a>). Your domain and your own
      content are yours whatever happens.
    </p>
    <h3>Hosting when you cancel a build</h3>
    <p>
      If I'm hosting the website when you cancel the build, I won't assume
      you want hosting and care to continue. Tell me in your cancellation
      email whether you'd like it to. If you don't confirm that you do, it
      ends as the cancellation arrangements for hosting and care in your
      quote set out.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of this affects your rights if work hasn't been done with
      reasonable care and skill (see{" "}
      <a href="#refunds">refunds and your rights</a>). Personal buyers also
      have the 14-day cancellation right described{" "}
      <a href="#cancel-14">below</a>.
    </p>
  </section>


  <section id="maintenance">
    <h2>Hosting and care</h2>
    <p>
      Hosting and care is optional, and separate from your build. If you'd
      like me to host and look after your website, your quote sets out what
      it costs, the work it includes, any domain costs, and how it can be
      cancelled.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        It starts when I start hosting your website, including while you're
        still paying for the website.
      </li>
      <li>
        It's separate from the build price and doesn't count towards paying
        it off.
      </li>
      <li>
        It's cancelled under the arrangements in your quote. Finishing or
        paying for your build doesn't end it. If you cancel the build, see{" "}
        <a href="#early">hosting when you cancel a build</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Work that your quote doesn't include, such as new pages, redesigns and
      new features, is quoted separately and only goes ahead if you agree.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cancelling hosting and care never means losing a website you've
      already paid for, and it doesn't cancel any payments still due for
      the build.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="backups">
    <h2>Backups</h2>
    <p>
      If your hosting and care includes backups (your quote says whether it
      does), I back up your website's code and the files stored in its
      repository once a week, and keep the copies with a cloud storage
      service.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Timing:</strong> a check runs once a day and takes a backup
        when one is due, aiming to create a backup each week. If the
        computer that runs them is switched off, asleep or offline, the
        backup runs late, usually at the next daily check it can complete.
        A missed week isn't recreated afterwards.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Upload:</strong> a backup needs that computer to be connected
        to the internet and the storage service to be available. If an upload
        fails, it's retried, and otherwise tried again at the next daily
        check.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>What's covered:</strong> the website's code and the files
        saved in its repository.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>What isn't covered:</strong> databases, emails, third-party
        services (such as booking or form tools), account settings (such as
        your domain registrar account), passwords and other secrets, and any
        changes that haven't been saved to the repository.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>How long copies are kept:</strong> weekly copies for about 8
        weeks and one copy a month for about 6 months. After that they're
        removed automatically and permanently deleted within a further 30
        days.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Backups make it possible for me to try to restore your website if
      something goes wrong. I can't guarantee that a restore will succeed,
      or how long it will take. That doesn't change my duty to provide
      hosting and care with reasonable care and skill, or
      any of your rights under the law (see{" "}
      <a href="#refunds">refunds and your rights</a>).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="own-hosting">
    <h2>Using your own hosting</h2>
    <p>
      You don't have to host with me. If you use your own hosting, you don't
      pay me for hosting and care. Once the full build price is paid, I'll
      provide the deliverables listed in your quote and basic setup
      instructions.
    </p>
    <p>
      I don't provide the files, or transfer ownership, before then, as
      described in{" "}
      <a href="#ownership">what becomes yours, and when</a>. Choosing your
      own hosting doesn't cancel any payments still due for your website.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="handover">
    <h2>Handover</h2>
    <p>
      When I'm hosting a website you've paid for and that hosting is ending,
      I'll send you, before hosting ends:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>a ZIP file of the deliverables listed in your quote</li>
      <li>basic instructions for putting it online with another host</li>
      <li>
        a note of anything that needs moving separately or can't be
        transferred, such as a database, a third-party service, a licence
        or an account in my name
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="after-hosting">
    <h2>After hosting ends</h2>
    <p>
      Once hosting has ended and the handover is complete, I keep a recovery
      copy of your website for 30 days, in case anything goes wrong with the
      move. After that I delete it from my active storage. Backup copies
      made while I was hosting your website are deleted automatically when
      they reach the end of the retention periods in{" "}
      <a href="#backups">backups</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This doesn't cover business records I have to keep, such as quotes,
      agreements and invoices. Those are kept for the periods set out in
      the <a href="/privacy/#retention">privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="domain">
    <h2>Your domain and content</h2>
    <p>
      Your domain, photos, text and logo are always yours, whatever happens.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>If you already have a domain,</strong> you keep it and stay
        in control of it. I only need enough access to point it at your
        website.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>If you need a new one,</strong> I'll register it with you as
        the named owner (the registrant).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cost:</strong> your quote sets out any domain costs while I
        host your website.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>When hosting with me ends,</strong> I'll help you take over
        the domain: either by moving it into a registrar account of your
        own, or by giving you its transfer code so you can move it to
        another provider. From then on, renewing it is up to you.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="late">
    <h2>If a payment is late</h2>
    <ol class="pp-steps">
      <li>
        I'll email you a reminder saying what's overdue, what I'll pause if
        it's still unpaid, and the date that would happen. That date will be
        at least 14 days after the reminder.
      </li>
      <li>
        If you pay before then, nothing else happens. If something's wrong,
        reply to the reminder and we'll sort it out.
      </li>
      <li>
        If it's still unpaid on that date, I may pause unfinished work on
        your website and hold back any handover (including a cancellation
        handover) until it's paid.
      </li>
      <li>
        If I'm hosting your website, I may also suspend hosting, which takes
        the website offline.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      If your website is hosted somewhere else, I won't access, change or
      disable it because of a late payment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pausing work and suspending hosting are temporary. They don't delete
      your website, they don't end our agreement, and they don't add any
      charges. Once the overdue amount is paid, I'll restart the work and,
      if I'd suspended hosting, put the website back online.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pausing work, suspending hosting, cancelling and deleting data are
      separate things. The agreement only ends as described in these terms,
      and website data is only deleted as described in{" "}
      <a href="#after-hosting">after hosting ends</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="refunds">
    <h2>Refunds and your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Whether you buy for a business or for personal use, the law requires
      my work to be carried out with reasonable care and skill. If something
      isn't, tell me and I'll put it right. If that isn't possible, you may
      be entitled to a price reduction or a refund.
    </p>
    <p>
      Beyond those rights, I don't offer an extra refund if you simply change
      your mind about work that's been completed and supplied properly.
      Nothing in these terms takes away or limits any rights you have by
      law.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cancel-14">
    <h2>14-day cancellation (personal buyers)</h2>
    <p>
      If you're buying for personal use and you accept a quote by email,
      phone or anywhere other than my business premises, you can cancel
      within 14 days of accepting it. You don't need to give a reason. To
      cancel, email me at{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${contact.email}`}>{contact.email}</a> with a clear
      statement that you're cancelling.
    </p>
    <p>
      I won't start work during those 14 days unless you ask me to in
      writing. If you ask me to start and then cancel within the 14 days,
      you pay a proportionate amount for the work done up to when you tell
      me you're cancelling, and I'll refund anything you've paid beyond that
      within 14 days.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you ask me to start early and the work is completely finished
      within the 14 days, you lose the right to cancel once it's finished,
      but only if you confirmed in writing, when asking me to start, that
      you understood this.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="contact">
    <h2>Questions and complaints</h2>
    <p>
      If something isn't right, email me at{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${contact.email}`}>{contact.email}</a> and I'll do my
      best to sort it out. For how I handle your personal information, see
      the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also write to me at the{" "}
      {business.addressLabel.toLowerCase()} on the{" "}
      <a href="/business-details/">business details</a> page.
    </p>
  </section>
</LegalLayout>
